# Rust coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to modern Rust
(2021/2024 edition) idiom, following the Rust API Guidelines. Applies only
to `*.rs` files.

## Naming and structure

- A crate's public surface is what `lib.rs` re-exports; keep details
  `pub(crate)` or private rather than every module `pub`.

## Ownership, borrowing, and errors

- Borrow (`&T`/`&mut T`) unless the callee keeps the value; `&str`/`&[T]`
  parameters over `&String`/`&Vec<T>`.
- Resolve a borrow-checker conflict by restructuring ownership, splitting the
  borrow, or shortening a lifetime; `.clone()` only when a duplicate is
  needed, with a comment when non-obvious.
- `Result<T, E>` for anything that can fail, propagated with `?` rather than
  a `match` that re-returns `Err`; `Option<T>` for genuine absence, not
  instead of `Result`.
- No `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` on a `Result`/`Option` built from fallible or
  untrusted input in production code without a stated invariant; use `?`, a
  `match`, or `unwrap_or`/`unwrap_or_else`/`ok_or`.
- `thiserror` for a library's own error enum (`#[error("...")]`,
  `#[source]`/`#[from]`); `anyhow::Result` with `.context()`/`.with_context()`
  in application/binary code.

## Traits and generics

- Narrowest trait bound the function needs (`AsRef<str>` over `&String`,
  `Iterator<Item = T>` over `Vec<T>`).
- Return `impl Trait` over `Box<dyn Trait>` unless runtime polymorphism is
  needed.
- Derive `Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, etc.; hand-write only when the
  derived behavior would be wrong for the type's invariants.

## Iterators and control flow

- An iterator chain over an indexed loop when it reads as clearly; do not
  force a chain that reads worse than a `for` loop.
- Match every variant of your own enum explicitly; no catch-all `_ =>` arm
  where a new variant should force call sites to be revisited.

## Async conventions (when the crate uses async)

- No blocking operation (`std::thread::sleep`, synchronous file/socket read,
  a `std::sync` primitive held across `.await`) inside an `async fn` on a
  multi-threaded executor; use `tokio::time::sleep`, `tokio::fs`,
  `tokio::sync::Mutex`, or `tokio::task::spawn_blocking`.
- Keep a `Mutex` guard's scope short, never across an `.await`;
  `tokio::sync::Mutex` when the lock must be held across an await,
  `std::sync::Mutex` when it never is.

## Formatting and lints

- Format with `rustfmt` (`cargo fmt`); do not hand-format around a
  configured formatter.
- `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`; fix at the root cause, not with a blanket
  `#[allow(...)]`.
